  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x+7}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6x+7}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+6x+7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+6x+7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=-1.$",
